From: Andrei-Alexandru Bleortu <me@andrei-z.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mirror: read the config command from the kernel buffer the handler is given

Since Linux 5.8 a sysctl proc_handler receives the written text in kernel
memory (commit 32927393dc1c, "sysctl: pass kernel pointers to
->proc_handler"). nss_mirror_ctl_config_handler() still copies it with
copy_from_user(), which refuses a kernel address, so every write to
/proc/sys/dev/nss/mirror/config fails with EFAULT ("Bad address") before
the command is parsed: no mirror instance can be created, configured or
enabled from userspace. Copy it with memcpy(), as the match client's
handler does since 0032-match-fix-procfs-read-write.
---
--- a/mirror/nss_mirror_ctl.c
+++ b/mirror/nss_mirror_ctl.c
@@ -755,7 +755,7 @@
  *	Mirror sysctl config handler.
  */
 static int nss_mirror_ctl_config_handler(compat_const struct ctl_table *ctl, int write,
-		 void __user *buf, size_t *lenp, loff_t *ppos)
+		 void *buf, size_t *lenp, loff_t *ppos)
 {
 	char *buffer, *pfree;
 	char * nextarg;
@@ -778,10 +778,10 @@
 
 	pfree = buffer;
 
-	if (copy_from_user(buffer, buf, count)) {
-		vfree(pfree);
-		return -EFAULT;
-	}
+	/*
+	 * A sysctl handler is given the written text in kernel memory.
+	 */
+	memcpy(buffer, buf, count);
 
 	nextarg = nss_mirror_ctl_read_nextarg(&buffer);
 	if (!nextarg) {
